lynxpm (0.8.3-1) unstable; urgency=high

  * **daemon: v0.8.1's process-group stop was incomplete.** Apps that
    relocate themselves into a fresh session/pgroup after fork
    (bash's `&` on Debian/Ubuntu job-control-disabled shells, any
    child that calls setsid(), gunicorn workers in pre-fork mode)
    escaped the kill(-pid, sig) and were left as orphans — exactly
    the bug 0.8.1 was meant to fix, now caught end-to-end by the
    regression smoke shipped in 0.8.2.
    gracefulKill now walks /proc up front, collects every descendant
    whose ppid-chain ends at the supervised PID, and signals them
    leaves-first *before* touching the parent. The walk runs before
    any signal is sent so orphans cannot race the kill by reparenting
    to init between signal delivery and descendant discovery. Pgroup
    signalling is kept as a cheap first-pass for well-behaved trees.
  * tests: the Go regression now forces the child into a new session
    with setsid(1), so the test fails on every platform if the /proc
    walk regresses — not only on shells that happen to relocate
    background jobs.

 -- Jaro-c <75870284+Jaro-c@users.noreply.github.com>  Sun, 19 Apr 2026 21:22:00 -0500

lynxpm (0.8.2-1) unstable; urgency=medium

  * ci: add an end-to-end regression guard for the v0.8.1
    process-group stop fix inside the Debian smoke test. The Go
    unit test already covers the daemon logic, but it exercises a
    source-built binary inside the test harness; this new smoke
    step runs against the installed .deb under a real user-mode
    lynxd, starts a bash wrapper that backgrounds a `sleep`,
    stops the wrapper via `lynxpm stop`, and asserts the child
    PID is also dead. Any future regression of Setpgid /
    kill(-pid, sig) now trips the `Install (debian:*, ubuntu:*)`
    matrix, not just the unit tests.

 -- Jaro-c <75870284+Jaro-c@users.noreply.github.com>  Sun, 19 Apr 2026 21:00:00 -0500

lynxpm (0.8.1-1) unstable; urgency=high

  * **daemon: 'lynxpm stop' would orphan child processes.** The
    supervised app was spawned without Setpgid, so the SIGTERM (and
    fallback SIGKILL) emitted by Stop only reached the wrapper PID
    that Lynx tracked. Anything the wrapper had fork()+exec()'d —
    next-server, gunicorn pre-fork workers, bash backgrounded jobs —
    survived as orphans, kept the listening socket bound, and caused
    EADDRINUSE on the next Start. Fixed by spawning every supervised
    process as its own process-group leader and signalling the whole
    group with kill(-pid, sig); affects every isolation mode (self,
    sandbox; dynamic was already covered by systemd's cgroup kill).
  * ci: a couple of older command files (list, start, apply, scale,
    delete, flush, plus the newly-edited lifecycle commands) were
    not formatted to the project's gofmt + golines (max-len 120)
    style. Rewrapped; the v0.8.0 release tag itself shipped the
    behaviourally-identical binaries before this fix landed.

 -- Jaro-c <75870284+Jaro-c@users.noreply.github.com>  Sun, 19 Apr 2026 20:48:00 -0500

lynxpm (0.8.0-1) unstable; urgency=medium

  * cli: lifecycle commands (stop, restart, reload, reset, delete,
    flush) gain --json batch reports and namespace bulk selectors.
    Operate on a whole namespace with `--namespace prod` or the
    `prod:*` selector (quote the glob); `*` / `*:*` target every
